Input: {"question": "In which significant event was Optical filters complementary angular blocking regions involved?", "documents": [{"doc_id": "d1", "text": "**The Unseen Guardians of Light: Unraveling the Mystery of Optical Filters with Complementary Angular Blocking Regions**\n\nIn the realm of optics, where light and darkness converge, a revolutionary innovation has been quietly shaping the course of human progress. The story begins with a peculiar patent, US 11366257, which holds the key to unlocking the secrets of optical filters with complementary angular blocking regions. This narrative delves into the intricate world of light manipulation, where the subtle dance of angles and blocking regions has far-reaching implications. At the heart of this tale lies the concept of optical filters, devices that selectively allow or reject specific wavelengths of light. These filters are the unsung heroes of modern technology, enabling applications as diverse as telescopes, microscopes, and even smartphone cameras. However, as the demands of modern technology continue to escalate, the need for more sophisticated filtering mechanisms has become increasingly pressing. Enter the pioneers of optical innovation, who have been working tirelessly to push the boundaries of light manipulation. Their groundbreaking discovery revolves around the concept of complementary angular blocking regions, a novel approach that has transformed the landscape of optical filtering. To grasp the significance of this innovation, let us first delve into the world of angular blocking regions. Imagine a filter as a gatekeeper, tasked with controlling the flow of light. Traditional filters rely on a single blocking region, which rejects light at a specific angle. However, this approach has its limitations, as it can lead to reduced transmission efficiency and increased losses. The genius of complementary angular blocking regions lies in its ability to overcome these limitations. By incorporating two or